    "q-124477": {
      "dom_id": "step-q124477",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "A cooler contains $10$ bottles of sports drink: $4$ lemon-lime flavored, $3$ orange flavored, and $3$ fruit-punch flavored. Consider the following scenarios:\n\n1\\. Jack randomly grabs a bottle and drinks it. Then he repeats the process once more. Let $F$ be the event \"the first selected bottle is fruit-punch flavored\" and let $L$ be the event \"the second selected bottle is lemon-lime flavored.\"\n\n2\\. Jack randomly grabs a bottle, returns the bottle to the cooler, and then mixes up the bottles. Then he repeated the process once more. Let ${O}_{1}$ be the event \"the first selected bottle is orange flavored\" and let ${O}_{2}$ be the event \"the second selected bottle is orange flavored.\"\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $F$ and $L$ are dependent\n\nII. $F$ and $L$ are independent\n\nIII. ${O}_{1}$ and ${O}_{2}$ are independent",
      "worked_solution": "Let's consider the situations in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. The events $F$ and $L$ are dependent because the first bottle is not replaced after Jack drank it. So, the number of lemon-lime flavored bottles is influenced by the result of the first choice, which then influences the result of the second choice.\n\n- Statement III is true. The events ${O}_{1}$ and ${O}_{2}$ are independent because the result of the first choice does not influence the second choice.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
